From b5ec645cb48da652052ff011d889d02c9fada5a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 20:10:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add the Verification Network: real measurement channels
 on the live path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Builds the verification layer into something that actually measures, using
free and open tools only, without touching the three protected demo fixtures
(croptop generation, kurti CSV, striped-dress CSV) — all three still resolve
through the exact-hash gate and return before the live path runs, regression-
verified after every change.

New measurement channels (services/verification_math.py, worker endpoints):

- C2 Colour fidelity — garment-masked LAB k-means palettes compared with
  CIEDE2000 in the textile industry's kL=2 parameterisation, per-view
  (front/front, back/back, closeup/closeup) with a median across views so one
  occluded view cannot dominate. Garment located by a declared-category body-
  region band plus interior/border cluster voting; closeups skip masking and
  chromatic normalisation entirely (gray-world on a frame that IS the garment
  bleaches the colour being verified). Validated on the demo set: identical
  sets score delta-E 0.0, the known-bad striped-dress catalog scores 9.4,
  different products 8.6-14.

- C3 Print geometry — 2-D FFT dominant spatial period measured in cycles per
  garment width, which camera distance cannot fake, plus gradient texture
  energy for print coverage. The striped-dress mismatch measures as a 0.20
  period ratio (5x scale difference); same-garment controls measure 0.95-1.05.
  Solid garments report "not applicable", never "match".

- C1 Identity — DINOv2-S/14 (same-object) and FashionCLIP (same-category)
  cosines served separately and as one agreement-weighted family score with a
  spread field, so fusion can treat the correlated embedding family as a
  single evidence slot and dampen it when the models disagree.

Worker v2 (services/ai_server.py):
- All heavy imports (torch, open_clip, timm, rembg, imagehash) are guarded.
  The worker now boots with just numpy+Pillow+FastAPI and serves the math
  channels — previously a missing torch stack crashed the whole process.
- /health reports a per-capability map; new status "math_only".
- New endpoints: /channel/color, /channel/texture, /channel/identity.

Fusion v2 (calculateNetworkFusion): multiplies likelihood ratios only across
channels with independent physics — identity (one slot for the whole
embedding family, falling back to the legacy raw CLIP score when the channel
did not run), colour, print geometry, pHash, and the deterministic attribute
comparison computed from the pre-channel comparison snapshot so channel
evidence is never double-counted. Missing signals stay missing;
confidence_tier reflects coverage.

Pipeline integration (routes/verify.js): channels run strictly on the live
path after the fixture gate, append seller-readable comparison rows and
verdict issues, and a failed ViT/CLIP attribute extraction no longer forces
UNVERIFIED when measured channel evidence exists — it becomes a coverage note.
The worker-availability gate and both health checks now accept a math-only
worker as degraded-but-usable instead of down.

Verified end-to-end with the worker in math-only mode: the known-mismatched
striped-dress listing submitted as new anchors is BLOCKED at 0.4% fused
probability with named causes (colour delta-E 9.4, print scale 0.20, geometry
length); the same garment on both sides passes at 99.5% with an honest
"partial" confidence tier. All three demo fixtures byte-identical; e2e suite
21/21; frontend builds.

Also: scripts/fetch_models.py (one-command deep-channel weight fetch — HF
downloads are blocked in this build environment, so weights install on the
target machine), scripts/calibrate.py (threshold calibration on labelled
pairs incl. same-category hard negatives), requirements.txt split into
math-tier and deep-tier, README channel documentation, frontend fusion card
renders the per-channel breakdown.

